	      >group that would go harass the nude bathers or beat up gays 
	      
In general, the desire not to be killed is much stronger than
the desire to kill a stranger, even a gay stranger. Gays would
be willing to pay as much as necessary, up to and including
their entire net worth not to be killed. I doubt if even the
most rabid gay basher would go much beyond 3%. As a result the
PPA protecting gays would be much stronger than the one that
wants to kill them.
	      
	     >whatever virtues this anarcho-capitalist utopia thing might
	     >have, freedom from coercion is not one of them.
	     
I do wish you'd stop using the straw man that were so stupid
that we think a society without coercion is possible. Force is
what PPA's are all about for goodness sake.
	     
	   >It might in fact be a fairly brutal, tense, and unpleasant system. 
	   
Brutal compared to what, states? Governments, those paragons of
charity and morality, those defenders of the weak and powerless,
the source of all virtue, have butchered hundreds of millions of
people this century alone, often their own citizens. We'd have
to work very hard to break that record.
	   
	   >I sure wouldn't want to rush into the  experiment. Personally.
	   
I don think we have a choice. Like it or not a change to
Anarcho-capitalism is inevitable . Not because somebody's
rhetoric convinces people of it's benefits and certainly not
because politicians change the law but because of technology.
Modern communication, cryptography and untraceable digital cash
will make it increasingly difficult to collect taxes.  The
tribute that could still be extracted like property taxes and
building permits would have to be increased to astronomic levels
and collected with a heavy hand, a tax revolt will follow.
Without money government will grind to a halt. I think the
Anarchy that follows will be similar to Friedman's, I sure hope
so for I realize not every Anarchy is superior to government.
	   
