From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Tue, 6 Dec 1994 10:30:33 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-12-209] FICTION: Aristoi (daemons)

Phil Goetz wrote:
>
>The daemons are normal mental processes.  They are different parts or
>aspects of a person's mind.  We have them now, but have not learned to
>control them.  The Aristoi can call them up when they want to use a
>certain aspect of their personality, and shut them up when they want to.

Many authors have described how their characters sometimes seems to come
alive, and possess their own goals, feelings and plans. The same is true
for many roleplayers (I think studying the characters of a person can give
valuable insights into his or her psychology), not to mention the
semi-independent entities in dreams. Although these persons are
implemented within our minds, they can show surprising independence or
even behave contrary to the wishes of the main personality (the classic
example is the Tulpas of Tibetan mysticism). Most importantly, sometimes
give very valuable insights. 

The problem is handling them, this is currently more an art than a
science. Ceremonial magick deals with them as if they were spirits,
manipulating them using symbols (IMHO a crude but promising system for
reprogramming the subconscious layers). Psychology seems to mainly want to
study them and find ways to reintegrate wayward or destructive
subpersonalities into the main personality. 


>I have mental inconsistencies:  the idealist in me wants to believe in
>absolute moral truths, but the materialist in me denies them.  These are
>two different daemons.  Part of me enjoys Mystery Science Theater 3000,
>and part of me thinks it's a waste of time.  These are separate daemons.
>(I think the "root personality" of an Aristoi is just the biggest daemon.)

The idea that we have just a single personality (and consciousness) is an
old myth. We are collectives of different entities (on many levels too),
which work together so well we don't notice that we are plural. However,
this illusion can be disturbed in many ways, especially when different
entities are so different or contradictory that they become visible. The
root personality of an Aristoi is probably a very well integrated mind
(probably more integrated than our current minds). 

I have experimented with "splitting off" subpersonalities for discussion
purposes (like I mentioned in the "Virtual Mentors" thread some time ago).
Its quite interesting to see how different parts of my mind are very keen
on finding faults in my theories. After all, they have access to my
subconscious fears and doubts. However, its always important to be in
control over your subpersonalities, and not take them seriously. Just
because one personality happens to want to become a serial killer doesn't
mean that you have to be one... :-)

