From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Tue, 6 Dec 1994 11:22:21 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-12-211] upgrading

Mark Fischer wrote:
>I am curious. With all the talk of uploading, enhanced abilities,
>etc., any discussion seems to presume these new abilities can be
>managed. Considering that the human brain is a hack, (With 3 distinct
>layers, reptilian, mammalian, and the most recently evolved,
>consciousness providing layer), and considering that the lower two
>regions are an abundant source of irrational behavior (that are
>tolerated, even expressed in the upper); has anyone pondered the
>stability of this structure were it to be upgraded?

This is an important issue I feel often get lost in the technical debate. 
Its probably hard, but the problem of handling great changes of existence 
has many more applications than just uploading (just learning how to cope 
in a society which changes all the time would be a most valuable skill).


>  It simply seems to beg credulity to think we can slap some
>add-on memory, neurons with a faster clock, maybe some sensors
>for the other parts of the E-M spectrum, etc. on to the existing
>architecture, and expect _anything_ that is actually stable from
>the result. More likely, we'll end up with lots of "unexplained
>application error"'s.

Fortunately the nervous system is fairly resistant to crashes. It will 
just be very confused. 

As I see it, we must tackle several problems here, and learn to understand
different levels. First there are the fundamental biological/neurological
questions of how do neurons and the brain work, which is needed to
interface with them on a physical level, not to mention important for
uploading, cognitive enchancement drugs etc.  The next level is to learn
what happens at the intermediate levels, how is information processed and
stored. The third level deals with low-level mental processes, the
subconscious and simple thought. The fourth level deals with the conscious
mind and high level mental processes.

To upload someone, we just need to know level 1 very well, and then use 
the required technology. To learn how to cope with this huge 
transformation, we need to understand more (but not necessarily all) 
about level 4. Lots of enchancements are possible on the various levels, 
and the pursuit of these would both spur further research and give 
valuable intermediate profits.

For example, a better understanding of the first level would give use 
much better drugs and ways of curing neuronal damage. It is possible that 
we could find ways to optimize brain chemistry.

Level two is even more interesting. By influencing different parts of the 
brain, we can control mood and cognitive processes a lot. This is more 
brute-force methods than using the more software-oriented layer above, 
but probably have more profound effects. Typical applications would be to 
find "shortcuts" to influence this activity (like mentally controlling 
alertness, mood or attention), memory enchancement techniques and the 
complex issues of linking new senses. Biofeedback works on this level. 

Level three is more programmable than the previous levels, which require 
some sort of hardware change in most cases. The subconscious mind and 
mental agents are not directly accessible, but can often be manipulated 
using various "tricks" (this is the root of my interest in ceremonial 
magick, which essentially is a codified form of programming some agents 
in a primitive way). By learning more efficient means to manipulate them 
(for example by developing a better mental "vocabulary" or conditioning 
certain responses) we could start to control even subconscious processes 
much better. Other applications would be Aristoi-style daemons, 
background processes, subconscious mnemotechnics and detecting common 
logical flaws (such as the human tendency to not notice Bayesian 
probabilities or mistaking implications for equivalences). 

The possible enchancements on level 4 are even more diverse, and 
currently the level where most activity in this area goes on. There are a 
multitude of methods which could be improved upon. Personality 
enchancement, more efficient ways to think, creativity training etc. This 
is probably the level most affected by the drastic changes in the 
uploading scenario (together with level 1 of course, and some emotional 
changes on level 3).

Note that we don't need to wait for any "super-technology" to start 
working on this, we can start right now with the higher levels! 

